# .cursorrules

## Rules for this repo

Short, direct versions of the same working agreements documented elsewhere
in this repo — read AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md for the longer explanation of
any of these, plus a few extra house rules below.

- Do not commit until `npm test` comes back green. No exceptions for "it's
  just a small change" — small changes break suites too.
- A good commit message says what changed and why, not just "fix stuff." If
  you can't summarize the change in one line, it's probably two commits
  pretending to be one.
- One idea per pull request. If it touches two unrelated things, split it
  into two PRs instead of bundling them together for convenience.
- Destructive changes — deletes, drops, force-pushes — need a check-in
  first. Ask, then act, never the other way around, even under a deadline.
- If a function is doing three things, it should probably be three
  functions. Long functions hide bugs in the middle where nobody looks
  closely enough to notice.
- Lint clean, always — no PR should carry unresolved warnings into review.
  Fix them before you ask for eyes, not after.
- Don't introduce a new dependency for something the standard library
  already covers. Check `Intl`, `URL`, and friends before reaching for npm.
- Match the existing indentation style in each file you touch, even when it
  differs from your editor's default settings — consistency beats personal
  preference here.
- Name branches after the ticket they close so anyone scanning the branch
  list can tell what's in flight without opening each one.
- Don't leave `console.log` debugging statements in committed code — strip
  them out before you open the PR, not after someone flags it in review.
- Delete a branch once its PR merges. A remote full of months-old dead
  branches makes it hard to tell what's actually still being worked on.
- Never commit a secret, even in a comment or a "temporary" test fixture.
  If one leaks, rotate it — deleting the line later does not undo the leak.
- Read the actual error message before asking in chat. Half the time it's
  already sitting right there in the terminal output, unread.
- Search for an existing helper before writing a new validator, formatter,
  or retry wrapper. Duplicate implementations of the same logic just mean
  twice the bugs to fix later, and only one copy ever gets the fix.
- Squash fixup commits before requesting review; a clean history is much
  easier to bisect later when something breaks in production.
- Prefer named exports over default exports so a rename doesn't silently
  break an import somewhere else in the codebase.
- Write the test for a bug fix first, watch it fail, then fix the code —
  it's the only way to prove the test actually catches the regression.
- Keep third-party API keys out of `.cursorrules` and every other tracked
  file. This file is documentation, not a secrets store.
- Run `npm run typecheck` locally before pushing; don't rely on CI to
  surface the first type error for you.
- Prefer relative imports within a package and workspace imports across
  packages — never mix the two styles in the same file.
- Reply to review comments within a day when you can. A PR sitting open
  for a week with unanswered feedback quietly blocks whoever's waiting on
  it, even if nobody says so out loud.
- Rebase feature branches onto the latest default branch before requesting
  review, so the diff a reviewer sees matches what will actually merge.
- Keep generated files (build output, lockfiles) out of hand-written pull
  requests — regenerate them in CI instead of committing them by hand.
- Open unfinished work as a draft PR, and only flip it to ready once it's
  actually done. A draft marked ready too early just wastes a reviewer's
  attention on something that isn't finished yet.
- Don't merge your own pull request without at least one approval, even
  for a change that feels obviously safe. The rule exists for the changes
  that turn out not to be obvious after all.
- Tag the relevant reviewer explicitly rather than posting into a channel
  and hoping someone notices — explicit beats implicit for anything time
  sensitive.
